  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 133 PM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nThe dry weather pattern will continue through at least Wednesday \nthis week as the aforementioned high pressure system will linger over \nthe central Great Lakes for the early part of the work week. By \nTuesday, the upper-level flow will become more zonal, with \nstrengthening southerly return flow around the high. Southwesterly \nsurface flow will support increasing warm air advection, resulting \nin high temperatures rising to the lower to middle 70s for Tuesday \nand Wednesday. The main focus for adjustments for this latest \nforecast was to lower the forecast dew points Monday through \nWednesday, considering the consensus blends tend to under perform\non the PBL mixing in dry air masses. Ultimately, this will \nresult in lower relative humidity.\n\nOur lone chance of any precipitation looks to come on Thursday \nnight, when a weak shortwave in the mid-levels is progged to \napproach the region. Mesoscale forcing will be enhanced due to a \nwarm frontal zone draped over Iowa. With the drier air in place, \nchances of measurable rainfall remain low, with both the NBM and \nLREF probabilities of measurable rain only around 10-30%. Outside of \nthis precip chance, dry conditions should prevail this week.",
